+# Tool Input Validation
+
+| Field  | Value                |
+| ------ | -------------------- |
+| Issue  | re-cinq/HALEngine#37 |
+| Status | In Progress          |
+
+Every tool declares a JSON Schema as its `inputSchema`, and that schema is handed verbatim to the providers so the model knows what to send. `ToolRegistry.execute` holds the model's reply to the same schema before any executor runs, so a tool that receives unexpected input fails in one predictable way instead of each executor guessing at its own defensive casts. This spec states that contract; the `execute` doc comment points here rather than restating it.
+
+## Validating a call
+
+- `ToolRegistry.execute` validates `input` against the resolved definition's `inputSchema` before calling the executor, and input missing a `required` property is answered with a `ToolResponse` whose `result` names the property as required, without the executor ever running ([validated by: returns a missing required property to the caller as a ToolResponse and never runs the executor](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L38)).
+- A property of the wrong declared type is rejected with a message naming the property path and the expected type, and neither the returned message nor any log line contains a value from the rejected input, since tool input is where identifying data enters the engine ([validated by: names the path and expected type of a wrong-typed property without echoing any input value](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L52)).
+- Validation enforces `required`, declared types and `enum` only: it does not inject `additionalProperties: false`, coerce types or apply defaults, and there is no opt-out flag.
+- `execute` still throws `Unknown tool: <name>` for a name that was never registered; only input the schema rejects becomes a returned `ToolResponse`.
+
+## Compiling schemas
+
+- A static definition's schema is compiled once, at `register` time, so a schema that cannot compile makes `register` throw with a message naming the tool, at startup rather than mid-conversation ([validated by: throws at register time, naming the tool, for a schema that cannot compile](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L75)).
+- A static definition's schema is not recompiled on later `execute` calls.
+- A function-valued `ToolDefinitionSource` is resolved on every call and its input validated against the schema it returns at that call, never against a stale cached one ([validated by: validates a function-valued definition against the schema it returns at that call, not a cached one](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L86)).
+
+## Inside the tool loop
+
+- A rejection is a tool result rather than an exception, so the model reads it on the next round and can retry within the `maxToolRounds` budget, `processMessage` resolves rather than rejects, and the corrected input reaches the executor exactly once with any extra property passed through unmodified ([validated by: lets the model read a rejection and retry within the tool loop, passing the corrected input through unchanged](../../src/orchestration/tools/registry.acceptance.test.ts#L105)).
+- The tool-round bound, not the validator, is what stops a model that keeps sending rejected input from spinning forever.
